{"author":{"id":"2eecc7d99b502c158aacb0868dca9358610edc02724d21fe49b0196402d3364e"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1784881568,"nonce":"czBQWQW9ZqcMlW4AlfncwP/Wvi4=","title":"ADR-0060 — Org settings surface (`docs/adr/0060-org-settings-connections-and-secret-management.md`).","message":"Make the Project **Secrets tab** the unified editor for **repo- and environment-scoped secret values**, by turning the currently read-only effective-status matrix (ADR-0037) into the editor. Rows are secret keys; columns are **`Repository default`** (the Project / `SecretScope::Repo` scope) plus one column per Environment. Editing a cell writes/deletes the value **at that scope** via the existing scoped `/v1/secrets` endpoints. Each cell always distinguishes **set-here / inherited / unset** (post-inheritance, so an inherited key never reads as missing), plus the ADR-0037 \"intentionally unset\" marker.\n\nThe Environments tab stays **rules-only** (approvers, allowed refs, timers) — secrets are one key-addressed namespace resolved by scope, not fragmented per-environment. User-facing copy says \"repo\"; the scope resolves against the Project + its optional Environment link. `SecretScope::Repo` is **not** renamed.\n\n\n- [ ] Matrix cells are editable: setting a value writes at that column's scope (repo default or a specific environment); clearing deletes at that scope.\n- [ ] Each cell shows set-here vs inherited vs unset correctly (inherited value visibly falls through from repo/org), with an \"intentionally unset\" toggle.\n- [ ] Environment-scoped values are settable from the matrix — closing the \"no UI to set an env secret\" gap.\n- [ ] Environments tab shows only protection rules; no secret editing lives there.\n- [ ] Functional test: setting an env-scoped override, then confirming resolution picks the env value while unset envs inherit the repo default.\n\n\nNone - can start immediately (lives in the existing Project Secrets tab, independent of the Settings shell).","files":null}]}